"""Simulated debate in English: people and, for each playable argument, a strong (f), weak (m) and bad (r) answer with why (fe, me, re).
Translated from tools/debate.py; Brazilian examples swapped for international ones where they were only illustrations."""

PESSOAS = [
    ('Mark', 'the uncle who runs the barbecue'),
    ('Julia', 'a friend from the gym'),
    ('Tony', 'a retired cattle farmer'),
    ('Bree', 'a nutrition student'),
    ('Rafael', 'a coworker'),
    ('Grandma Rose', 'who cooks for the whole family'),
    ('Peter', 'a cousin who studies philosophy'),
    ('Camila', 'a friend who posts her workouts'),
    ('Luke', 'a childhood friend'),
    ('Fiona', 'a biology teacher'),
    ('Diego', 'a neighbor who loves to argue'),
    ('Patricia', 'a boss who avoids controversy'),
]

OP = {
 'A4': dict(
  f="Plants respond to their environment, but they have no neurons or anything doing that job, and there's no evidence they feel. And if they did, eating plants directly would spare them: much of the world's cropland grows feed for animals.",
  fe='Separates responding from feeling and shows that, even granting the premise, the conclusion favors eating plants.',
  m="Science has already proven that plants feel absolutely nothing: without a brain there's no experience of any kind, and biologists settled this decades ago. Comparing a lettuce to a pig makes no sense at all.",
  me='It points the right way, but "proven" and "settled" are too strong, and it misses what matters: even if they did feel, eating animals would kill more plants.',
  r='I get the concern, but then you should go eat rocks, since apparently everything feels. Funny how people who never cared about a lettuce become defenders of plants the moment the subject is the steak on their plate.',
  re='Sarcasm and an accusation of bad faith end the conversation and explain nothing. The person walks away thinking you had no answer.'),
 'G2': dict(
  f="We need protein, not animal protein. Beans, lentils, soy, chickpeas and grains provide all the amino acids, if you eat a varied diet and enough of it. Nutrition bodies like the US Academy of Nutrition and Dietetics and the British Dietetic Association accept well-planned vegan diets.",
  fe='Corrects the confusion between nutrient and source, gives concrete examples and says who backs the claim.',
  m='Just look at nature: gorillas, bulls, horses and elephants are among the strongest animals on the planet, and they eat nothing but plants their whole lives. If they build tons of muscle with no animal protein, our bodies can obviously get everything they need from plants too.',
  me="False analogy: cattle and horses rely on microbes that digest grass, which we don't have. It's one of the weak arguments vegans should stop using.",
  r="It's true that we need protein, but animal protein causes cancer, and doctors already agree on that. People who eat meat every day are slowly poisoning themselves and their kids. The industry has hidden the data for decades, which is why almost no one hears about it.",
  re='An exaggeration the evidence does not support: the processed meat risk is real, but "poisoning" and the supposed consensus are false, and the conspiracy makes it worse. The person stops trusting the rest.'),
 'C1': dict(
  f="Being natural doesn't make something right: nature has disease and infanticide, and we use medicine and anesthesia without a second thought. And supermarket meat comes from animals bred and confined on an industrial scale, which is nothing like natural.",
  fe='Shows that "natural" is not a moral criterion and that, even if it were, today\'s animal farming would not fit it.',
  m="Actually, eating meat is what isn't natural: our earliest ancestors were vegetarian and lived on fruit, roots, leaves and seeds. Meat only entered the human diet very recently, and our bodies never truly adapted to it.",
  me='Disputes the wrong fact, and the claim is false: early humans ate meat. Even if it were true, it would accept that what is natural decides what is right.',
  r="Dying of an infection at thirty is natural too, so be consistent and stop taking medicine, wearing glasses and going to the dentist. But of course you won't: a hypocrite only cares about what's natural when it defends the barbecue.",
  re='The underlying idea is good, but the personal attack makes the person defend themselves instead of thinking.'),
 'J5': dict(
  f="For people who like it, it does taste good. The question is whether that pleasure justifies someone's life. No one would defend eating dogs because they're tasty. And much of the flavor comes from seasoning and cooking, which work on plants too.",
  fe='Concedes what is true, puts the weight on both sides and tests the principle with a case the person rejects.',
  m="It's true that many people like it, but that's no longer a problem: plant-based meat is now exactly the same as the real thing in flavor, texture and juiciness. In a blind test nobody can tell the difference, so you lose nothing by switching.",
  me='Many people do notice, and the broken promise becomes an argument against you. It also swaps the ethical question for one about taste.',
  r="Meat actually tastes of nothing: all the flavor comes from the salt, the garlic and the smoke. Try eating a steak boiled in plain water with no seasoning and see who can stand it. People who say they like meat really like seasoning and don't realize it.",
  re="It's false: meat has its own flavor, and meat eaters know it. Denying the obvious costs credibility."),
 'C2': dict(
  f="We're omnivores, and that's exactly why we can choose: we live well on plants. Being able to eat something isn't needing to, and anatomy gives no moral orders.",
  fe='Accepts the biological fact and shows it does not lead to the conclusion.',
  m="Actually, we're herbivores by nature: our canines are tiny, our intestines are long and we have no claws. The human body simply wasn't made to eat meat.",
  me='We are omnivores, and saying otherwise is a biology mistake. It also accepts the idea that anatomy decides morality.',
  r="Omnivore, huh? Then drop the knife and the stove and go hunt a cow with those little teeth; I'd like to see that. People who buy meat on a tray shouldn't talk about anatomy.",
  re="A taunt that doesn't answer the argument and turns the conversation into an ego contest."),
 'F1': dict(
  f="Maybe I would, just as a starving person might steal food, and no one concludes stealing is fine in everyday life. Extreme necessity justifies exceptions, but none of us is on a desert island.",
  fe='Answers honestly and shows that an extreme exception does not justify the everyday practice.',
  m="I'd never eat an animal, not even starving on an island. I'd rather die with a clean conscience than betray my principles, and anyone who takes ethics seriously would do the same. For me, no situation justifies it.",
  me="It sounds like purity and is hard to believe. It also accepts discussing the island, which has nothing to do with today's plate.",
  r="That's a stupid question: nobody lives on a desert island, and you know it. Anyone who has to invent a movie plot to defend what they eat has already run out of arguments in real life. It isn't even worth answering.",
  re='The question is common and deserves an answer. Calling it stupid and refusing to answer makes it look like you dodged it.'),
 'C5': dict(
  f="The lion has no choice: it's an obligate carnivore and doesn't deliberate. We can live well without eating animals and we understand what we do. That's why we don't judge the lion, and why we can be judged.",
  fe='Shows the morally relevant difference between the lion and us: choice and understanding.',
  m='If the lion is your example, remember that lions also kill the cubs of other males and take the females by force. Will you copy that too? Anyone who uses nature as a guide has to accept the whole package, not just the menu.',
  me="The point is valid as backup, but on its own it sounds like a taunt and doesn't explain why we differ from the lion.",
  r="You're not a lion; stop acting like a predator. A lion chases down its food on the savanna; you pick up a tray at the store and ask for it well done. That comparison only works for people who want to feel like the king of the jungle.",
  re='Ridicules instead of arguing, and the person still doesn\'t see which difference matters.'),
 'H2': dict(
  f="It's true that farming kills animals. But animal farming needs far more cropland, because the animals eat feed, and huge pastures. Eating plants directly uses less land and tends to cause fewer deaths.",
  fe='Concedes the fact and shows it favors eating plants.',
  m="That doesn't apply to vegans: our diet doesn't kill any animals and is 100% cruelty-free. Harvest deaths are a myth spread by the meat industry, and modern crop farming barely affects the animals living in the fields.",
  me="Promises a purity that doesn't exist and denies a true fact, the harvest deaths. Veganism speaks of reducing harm \"as far as is possible\".",
  r="It's true that some animals die in the harvest, but you only just remembered that, didn't you? That's an excuse from someone who doesn't want to change and looks for a flaw in others to keep eating meat without guilt.",
  re="Attacks the person's motives instead of showing what the fact, which is true, actually means."),
 'D1': dict(
  f="Doing something for a long time doesn't make it just: slavery lasted for centuries. Traditions change all the time, and cockfighting, once a widespread tradition, is now banned across the United States and the United Kingdom for its cruelty.",
  fe='Shows tradition is not a criterion and gives examples of traditions that changed precisely because of the animals.',
  m="Actually, we haven't always done it: people used to eat much less meat, and it only reached the table on feast days. Today's consumption is a recent invention of the industry, so the real tradition is eating very little meat.",
  me='It may be partly true, but it accepts that tradition decides what is right and only argues about the date.',
  r="There have always been ignorant people too, and that never made ignorance a virtue. Anyone who repeats \"it's always been this way\" is just admitting they never stopped to think and would rather let their grandparents decide for them.",
  re="A veiled insult that doesn't explain why tradition doesn't justify anything."),
 'E1': dict(
  f="Personal choices should be respected when they only affect the person choosing. Choosing between coffee and tea affects no one; choosing something that requires an animal's death affects someone who didn't choose. And talking about it isn't imposing.",
  fe='Accepts the principle of freedom and shows where it ends: when there is another victim.',
  m="It stops being personal when it affects others, and your choice affects everyone: animal farming is the biggest cause of deforestation and global warming. People who eat meat are destroying the planet my children will live on, so it's my business too.",
  me="The environmental impact is real, though overstated here, but it's a borrowed reason: it swaps the direct victim, the animal, for an argument that falls if the meat is \"sustainable\".",
  r="\"Personal choice\" is exactly what a murderer would say too: the victim was his and it was nobody else's business. Deep down the logic is the same, and the only difference between the two of you is that your victim can't call the police.",
  re='Compares the person to a murderer. Even if the logic has a point, they will only hear the insult.'),
 'B1': dict(
  f='"Humane" describes the way of killing, not a reason to kill. Even without pain, a young animal loses the life ahead of it. And in practice it often fails: birds are shackled while conscious, and pigs are stunned with a gas they try to avoid.',
  fe='Separates how the animal dies from the fact that it dies, and adds that even the method is not what people imagine.',
  m='Humane slaughter only exists on paper. In practice, every animal is killed fully conscious, with no stunning at all, because the line moves too fast and nobody inspects it. The label is only there to reassure the consumer, and the industry knows it.',
  me='It\'s an exaggeration: stunning is the legal rule in many countries, even though it fails and has exceptions. Anyone who knows the subject takes the sentence apart and ignores the rest.',
  r="Humane would be killing you the same way. Would you accept that? A blow to the head, a cut to the throat, all quick and done with great respect. If the idea bothers you, maybe the problem was never the method, but you haven't wanted to see it yet.",
  re='The comparison has a point, but said this way it sounds aggressive and pulls the focus away from the argument.'),
 'K1': dict(
  f="The slaughterhouse doesn't change production for one tray, but it does for many, and someone's purchase is always the one that triggers the next order. Since no one knows whether it'll be theirs, the average effect of each purchase is real. And when demand falls, production falls.",
  fe="Explains the expected-value reasoning without promising that each purchase saves a specific animal.",
  m='It does make a difference, and you can measure it: each vegan saves about 200 animals a year, which adds up to more than ten thousand over a lifetime. Every tray you leave on the shelf is one less animal at the slaughterhouse, and the math is that simple.',
  me='These figures circulate widely, but they vary with diet and method, and one tray less is not exactly one animal less. The precision is false.',
  r="If everyone thought like that, the world would be doomed: nobody would vote, nobody would save water, nobody would do anything. How selfish. That reasoning is typical of people who only lift a finger when they're sure they'll get the credit for the result.",
  re="A moralizing appeal that doesn't address the real doubt about the effect of one purchase."),
 'A15': dict(
  f="The question isn't whether a being can reason, but whether it can suffer. A pig's pain doesn't hurt less because it can't solve equations. If reason were the criterion, babies and people with profound intellectual disabilities could be used, and no one accepts that.",
  fe='Replaces the wrong criterion with the relevant one and shows the reason criterion would exclude humans we all protect.',
  m="Science has already shown that animals are as intelligent as we are. Pigs are smarter than three-year-olds, octopuses solve puzzles and crows use tools. If they reason the way we do, the difference you're pointing to simply doesn't exist.",
  me='There are comparisons on specific tasks, but generalizing overstates them. And it accepts the idea that intelligence decides who counts.',
  r="You're not being very rational yourself, and nobody puts you on a plate for it. Someone so proud of their own reason should start using it, instead of repeating the first sentence they heard somewhere just to justify today's lunch.",
  re='A personal attack: even with the seed of an argument in it, the person only hears the insult.'),
 'A8': dict(
  f="That sentence gives no reason; it just repeats which group the animal belongs to. If some trait matters, which one? Every trait people point to is missing in some human we still protect. If the answer is \"just being another species\", that's prejudice, not an argument.",
  fe='Asks for the criterion and shows that none separates all humans from all animals.',
  m="We're animals too: biology classifies humans as primates, and we share most of our DNA with other mammals. So saying \"they're just animals\" makes no sense at all, because the sentence applies equally to you, to me and to everyone here.",
  me="True in biology, but it doesn't answer: the person will just say \"yes, but humans are different\". The point is to ask which difference matters.",
  r="That's exactly what the Nazis said: pick a group, declare it worth less and do whatever you want with it. Slaughterhouses are today's concentration camps, and people who buy meat are playing the part of those who saw the smoke and preferred not to ask.",
  re='Holocaust comparisons offend and steer the conversation toward the comparison, not the animals.'),
 'A3': dict(
  f="Not knowing exactly where the line is doesn't erase clear differences: we don't know the exact day someone becomes elderly, and a baby still isn't elderly. Cows, pigs and chickens are clearly on the side of those who feel. In doubtful cases, like insects, the sensible course is caution.",
  fe='Answers the continuum fallacy and admits the real doubts without letting them erase the clear cases.',
  m="The line is simple and it's the same for everyone: anything with eyes can feel. If a being can look at you, there's someone in there, and that goes for the cow, the fish and the fly. Bacteria and plants have no eyes, so they're out, and the problem is solved.",
  me="Invents an arbitrary criterion that has nothing to do with feeling, and it's easy to knock down.",
  r="Bacteria? Seriously? You must be joking. You've spent your life eating cows, pigs and chickens without asking a single question, and now you're worried about microbes. When someone has to go down to bacteria to be right, the serious conversation ended long ago.",
  re="Mocks the question instead of explaining where the doubt is real and where it isn't."),
 'B2': dict(
  f="Death is a harm because it takes away everything the person would still have lived. That's why killing someone in their sleep, painlessly, is extremely serious. Animals also have a future of good experiences, and farmed animals are killed very young.",
  fe='Explains why death is a harm even without pain, with a human case no one accepts.',
  m="That premise is false: there's no such thing as painless death. Every animal suffers at slaughter, smells the blood, hears the others screaming and panics before dying. If death really were painless, this would be a different conversation.",
  me='Shifts to pain, which is not always present to the same degree, and accepts the premise that, without pain, everything would be fine.',
  r="So can I kill you in your sleep? You wouldn't feel a thing, you wouldn't even know, and by your logic nobody would have anything to complain about. Think carefully before you answer: if painless killing of someone asleep is acceptable, nothing stops you from agreeing.",
  re='The idea is right, but said in a provocative tone it sounds hostile and the person reacts to the tone.'),
 'B4': dict(
  f="A better life is better, no doubt. But the free-range animal is still killed young, and the male chicks are still discarded. And that meat is the exception: most farmed animals live in intensive systems.",
  fe='Acknowledges the improvement and shows what does not change: the death, and the fact that this meat is a minority.',
  m="It's true that the label looks nice, but free-range is all a lie, just marketing: the \"happy\" hen on the carton lives in the same crowded shed as the others, and they all suffer the same. The only difference is the price.",
  me='Labels exaggerate, but saying there is no difference at all is false, and anyone who has seen a better farm stops listening.',
  r='Happy right up until the knife, right? It must be a huge comfort to the animal to know it got to scratch around the yard first. But if paying more helps you sleep at night, go on believing in the smiling hen on the carton.',
  re='The sarcasm has a point, but on its own it sounds like a taunt, without explaining the argument.'),
 'K4': dict(
  f="The industry is responsible, and changing laws and subsidies matters. But it produces because people buy. You can do both: push for system change and change what you buy.",
  fe='Agrees with the right part and shows it does not replace individual choice.',
  m="The industry only makes what people ask for. It's all the buyer's fault: the consumer is in charge, and companies simply obey. Laws and subsidies change nothing as long as people keep paying.",
  me='As one-sided as the objection: it ignores the real weight of subsidies, advertising and laws.',
  r="That's an excuse from someone who doesn't want to give up steak. Blaming the industry is easy: you get to pose as a critic of the system and stay in line at the butcher's with a clear conscience.",
  re='Attacks the motive instead of answering the argument.'),
 'B3': dict(
  f="A good life doesn't give the right to take it; the better the life, the greater the loss. And the alternative to the slaughterhouse isn't a wolf, it's not being bred to die. No one would accept that sentence about a dog.",
  fe='Shows that a good life increases the loss and tests the principle with a case the person rejects.',
  m='No farmed animal has a good life: they are all tortured from birth to death, without exception. That story about well-treated animals on the family farm is just advertising, and anyone who believes it has never seen a farm up close.',
  me='Overgeneralizes: some farms are worse than others. The person thinks of the farm they know and dismisses everything you say.',
  r='A good life until they were murdered. Lovely. By that logic you can raise anyone with affection and then collect the bill with a knife. The animal must be so grateful; too bad nobody asked its opinion before dinner.',
  re='Irony that explains nothing and sounds contemptuous.'),
 'C4': dict(
  f="Even if meat helped our ancestors, that doesn't tell us what to do today: they also lived without medicine. And the claim itself is debated, with cooking and other foods also seen as important.",
  fe='Separates the past from the present moral choice and shows the premise is disputed.',
  m="That's a myth that has already been debunked: meat played no role at all in human evolution. The brain grew thanks to cooked roots and fruit, and serious scientists dropped the meat hypothesis a long time ago.",
  me='Goes beyond the evidence: its exact role is debated, but "no role" is too strong a claim.',
  r="Evolve? Look who's talking. If meat had done the brain that much good, you'd have a better argument than this one. It looks like evolution stopped for some of us right in front of the grill.",
  re='A personal attack with no argument.'),
 'C3': dict(
  f="Wanting isn't needing. Much of the taste for meat is learned from childhood and reinforced by salt, fat and seasoning. And desires don't justify causing harm: we have many cravings we don't follow when they hurt someone.",
  fe='Separates desire from justification and explains where part of the craving comes from.',
  m="That craving isn't natural; it's an addiction. Meat acts on the brain like a drug, with substances that cause dependence just like cigarettes, which is why it's so hard to quit. What you're feeling is withdrawal, and it passes in a few weeks.",
  me='Comparing it to a drug overstates things and has no solid basis; it sounds like an accusation.',
  r="A natural craving for corpses? Gross. Normal people see a dead animal on the road and look away, but your mouth waters. If that's natural for you, I'd rather not know what else you crave.",
  re="Disgust isn't an argument, and the person feels judged."),
 'D6': dict(
  f="Respecting a culture isn't accepting everything in it. You probably criticize bullfighting or cockfighting, and many places have banned them for cruelty. And the criticism applies mainly to our own culture.",
  fe='Shows the person already judges cultural practices and that the criticism is not aimed at other peoples.',
  m="This culture isn't even ours: eating meat every day is a colonizer thing, brought over with the cattle and the fences. Indigenous peoples lived in harmony with animals, and defending meat today is defending the legacy of colonization.",
  me="A historical generalization that doesn't hold up: many cultures eat animals. It also swaps the moral question for a political dispute.",
  r="Slavery was cultural too, and there were always people asking for it to be respected. You're using the same argument the plantation owners used, and a hundred years from now people will look at you the way we look at them today.",
  re='The logical point has something to it, but said like that it compares the person to slaveholders, and that is all they hear.'),
 'D7': dict(
  f='Veganism asks us to avoid exploitation "as far as is possible and practicable". An isolated people who depend on hunting are in a different situation from someone shopping at a supermarket. Using those with no choice to justify those who have one doesn\'t work.',
  fe="Recalls veganism's definition and separates necessity from choice.",
  m="Ethics applies to everyone. Indigenous peoples should be vegan too, no exceptions: killing an animal is wrong in the city or in the forest, and culture doesn't change that. If we make an exception for one people, everyone will want their own, and the principle disappears.",
  me="Ignores real living conditions, contradicts veganism's own definition and sounds like an imposition on vulnerable peoples.",
  r="You're not Indigenous; stop using them as an excuse. I bet you've never cared about those peoples in your life, and they only come up when the subject is your plate. The closest you get to hunting is the line at the butcher's.",
  re='The point has something to it, but the aggressive tone ends the conversation.'),
 'D3': dict(
  f="What brings the family together is the gathering, not the animal. The conversation and the full table carry on with other food, and traditions change all the time. These days there are dishes everyone can share.",
  fe='Separates the real value, the gathering, from what can be replaced.',
  m="Let's be honest: family barbecues are just an excuse to get drunk and gossip about whoever didn't come. Nobody is there for the affection, and the togetherness you describe exists more in the photos than in real life.",
  me='Dismisses the experience of the person and their loved ones. They will defend their family instead of thinking about the animals.',
  r="A family that gathers around a corpse isn't a family; it's a group of accomplices. If your love depends on a dead animal in the middle of the table, maybe it's worth asking what kind of bond that is and what it says about you.",
  re='Offensive and false. The person will feel attacked along with their loved ones.'),
 'E3': dict(
  f="Almost no one really believes morality is relative: you wouldn't think a mugging is wrong only in the victim's opinion. There's a difference between taste and harm: preferring chocolate ice cream is taste; making someone suffer isn't.",
  fe='Shows the person does not apply relativism to other cases and separates taste from harm.',
  m="It isn't a matter of opinion: science proves it's wrong to eat animals. Studies show they feel pain and are conscious, so the moral conclusion has already been demonstrated. Disagreeing with that today is like disagreeing that the Earth is round.",
  me='Science shows that animals feel, but the moral conclusion takes an ethical step. Confusing the two makes the reply easy.',
  r="Then anything goes, even what people might do to you. If everyone decides what's right, don't complain when someone decides it's right to take your wallet, or worse. Let's see how long that relativism lasts when you're the victim.",
  re="It becomes an implied threat and doesn't explain the problem with relativism."),
 'E2': dict(
  f="No one here talked about arresting meat eaters: the question is whether it's right, not whether it should be banned. Many wrong things aren't crimes. And the state already regulates what we eat and bans animal cruelty.",
  fe='Brings the conversation back to the moral question and shows the law already protects animals.',
  m="It should be banned, and it will be. The state already bans plenty of things that harm others, and meat is just next on the list. In a few decades eating animals will be a crime, and you'd better start getting used to the idea.",
  me="Confirms the person's fear and swaps a conversation about what is right for one about bans, which they will reject.",
  r="The state bans plenty of things you'd do, thank goodness. The people who complain most about rules are usually the ones who need them most, and I can imagine what you'd get up to if nobody were watching.",
  re="A personal insinuation that answers nothing."),
 'E10': dict(
  f="Nobody needs to be perfect, and veganism doesn't ask for that. But \"I do what I can\" holds if you're actually doing what you can, and changing what you eat is usually one of the easiest changes with the most impact.",
  fe='Agrees with the principle and shows it calls for exactly the change within reach.',
  m="Doing what you can isn't enough. If you're not vegan, you're doing nothing: cutting back, buying free-range or skipping meat on Mondays only ease your conscience. For the animals, either you've quit for good or you're still on the same side.",
  me='All-or-nothing discourages people and is false: those who cut back already cause less harm.',
  r="Do what you can, just don't stop killing. Funny how your \"possible\" always ends just short of the plate. Deep down you do what's comfortable, call it effort and still expect a pat on the back.",
  re='A direct accusation that puts the person on the defensive.'),
 'E15': dict(
  f="Thanks for being honest: you already agree on the most important part. The gap between knowing and doing is human, but it doesn't have to be permanent. You can start with one meal or one day a week. What makes it hardest for you?",
  fe="Acknowledges the concession, doesn't humiliate and offers a concrete next step.",
  m="Then you're a hypocrite, and I mean no offense: it's simply the accurate word for someone who knows something is wrong and keeps doing it. Admitting it changes nothing for the animals. Until you stop, your honesty is worth about as much as an empty apology.",
  me='It may even be a logical description, but labeling ends the conversation right when the person conceded the point.',
  r="Then don't complain when someone does the same to you. A person who knows they're wrong and carries on is worse than one who doesn't know, and one day the bill comes due. When it does, I hope you're treated as lazily as you treat animals.",
  re='A vague threat with no argument and no way forward.'),
 'F2': dict(
  f="I'd probably save the child, just as many people would save their own child rather than a stranger. But that doesn't give permission to use strangers. Choosing whom to save in a tragedy doesn't license hurting those who aren't in danger.",
  fe='Answers the question honestly and shows that a preference in an emergency does not justify exploitation.',
  m="I'd save the dog. It's more innocent: it has never harmed anyone, and humans are the species that does the most damage to the planet. Honestly, most of the time animals deserve our help more than people do.",
  me='Sounds like contempt for humans and reinforces the stereotype that vegans value animals over people.',
  r="That's a manipulative question; I won't answer it. You set up an absurd scenario just to corner me and then go around saying vegans hate people. Save that trick for someone who falls for it, and let me know when you want a real conversation.",
  re='Refusing to answer looks like dodging. The question has a good answer.'),
 'F3': dict(
  f="Wild animal suffering is a serious question, and some people study ways to reduce it without causing more harm. But whatever the answer, it doesn't favor eating animals: suffering in nature is no reason to cause more.",
  fe='Takes the question seriously and shows it does not support the conclusion.',
  m="It's a serious question, and the consistent answer is yes: we should get rid of all predators. If suffering is bad, it doesn't matter who causes it, so the right thing would be to phase out lions, wolves and hawks and leave only herbivores.",
  me='Such an intervention could cause more suffering, and the sentence sounds absurd. The topic calls for caution.',
  r="Lions aren't my problem. What happens on the savanna is nature's business, and I only care about suffering that has something to do with me. Besides, that question is just an attempt to change the subject, and I won't fall for it.",
  re='Contradicts the very concern with suffering and proves the accusation of inconsistency right.'),
 'G3': dict(
  f="Plant foods have all the essential amino acids, in different proportions. Over the day they complement each other, with no need to combine them in the same meal. Rice and beans are already an excellent combination.",
  fe='Corrects the myth with an everyday example.',
  m="Actually, plant protein is even better than animal protein: the body absorbs it more easily, it doesn't strain the kidneys and it comes without fat. Any single plant food already delivers more than meat does.",
  me='There is no basis for "better" in general: profile and absorption vary. Overstating it gives ammunition to people who know nutrition.',
  r='Protein is all the same, and that talk of "complete" and "incomplete" is a meat industry invention to sell steak. Dietitians repeat it because they were trained on materials paid for by meatpackers.',
  re='There are real differences in profile and digestibility. A conspiracy theory destroys credibility.'),
 'G4': dict(
  f="B12 is made by bacteria, not by animals. Farmed pigs and chickens get B12 in their feed, and cattle often get cobalt. Meat eaters depend on supplementation too, just indirectly. And we use iodized salt and vaccines without a second thought.",
  fe='Explains where B12 comes from and shows supplementation is already part of the meat supply chain.',
  m='All the B12 in meat comes from supplements. No animal makes this vitamin, and all of them, from chickens to cattle, get injections or fortified feed. Meat eaters are just taking the same pill I take, after it has passed through an animal.',
  me="An exaggeration: in ruminants, the bacteria in the rumen produce B12. The claim is easily refuted.",
  r="Vegans don't even need B12; it's a pharmaceutical industry scam to sell pills. Anyone who eats fresh vegetables and gets some sun has everything the body needs, and that deficiency only shows up in people who eat badly anyway.",
  re='False and dangerous: anyone who doesn\'t eat animal products needs a reliable source of B12.'),
 'G11': dict(
  f="What matters for muscle is the amount of protein and energy. A 2021 study with equal protein intake found no difference in muscle mass or strength between vegans and omnivores. There are elite vegan athletes in many sports.",
  fe='Uses specific evidence and examples without overstating.',
  m='You can, and even better: vegan athletes perform far better, and the documentary showed it clearly. They recover faster and have more stamina and more strength, which is why the great champions are giving up meat.',
  me='Documentaries sell conclusions the studies don\'t support. "Far better" is an exaggeration.',
  r="Athletes who eat meat are on steroids anyway. Those gym bodies you admire didn't come from chicken and sweet potato; they came from the pharmacy. Compare vegans with people who train clean and then we can talk.",
  re='An unfounded accusation that doesn\'t answer the question.'),
 'G12': dict(
  f="It's a stereotype: there are all kinds of vegans, including elite athletes. On depression, the studies are mostly cross-sectional and don't show what causes what. Deficiencies that affect mood can be prevented with planning.",
  fe='Undoes the stereotype and treats the evidence on depression honestly.',
  m="It's the opposite: vegans are healthier and happier, and it's been proven. Research shows less depression, more energy and more drive in people who take animal products off their plate. Meat is what leaves people sick and listless.",
  me='Flips the stereotype with another claim the evidence does not support.',
  r="The pale ones are people who live on barbecue and beer. Look around at the next family party: bellies, high blood pressure, cholesterol pills. If anyone here looks sick, it certainly isn't the person eating salad.",
  re='Answers a stereotype with a stereotype and gives offense.'),
 'H9': dict(
  f="It's true that some pasture can't grow crops. But that land produces little food: livestock uses about 80% of agricultural land and provides about 17% of calories. That land could return to native vegetation.",
  fe='Concedes the true part and shows the ratio between land used and food produced.',
  m="That doesn't hold up, because all pasture is cleared rainforest. There's no such thing as \"natural\" grazing land: wherever there are cattle today, there used to be forest. Every steak you eat is a piece of woodland that was cut down.",
  me='A false generalization: there are old pastures and pastures in other ecosystems. The exaggeration shifts the focus.',
  r="Cattle destroy everything they touch; that's a fact: they wreck the soil, the water and the climate. Any informed person knows it, and whoever defends pasture either hasn't read anything on the subject or has a rancher in the family.",
  re='A vague, exaggerated claim that is easy to rebut, with a personal jab at the end, and it also swaps the moral question for an environmental one.'),
 'J7': dict(
  f="Eating less meat is good and deserves credit: fewer animals die. But if killing without need is wrong, doing it less often is less wrong, not right. Cutting back is a great path, as long as it doesn't become the destination.",
  fe='Values the step and shows it does not answer the moral question.',
  m='A little meat is still meat, and it changes nothing. To the animal that died, it makes no difference whether you eat it every day or only on Sundays. Cutting back is just a way to feel good without really changing, and I see no reason to praise it.',
  me="It does change something: fewer animals die. Dismissing the reduction discourages people who are on the way.",
  r='A part-time killer, then. Congratulations on only killing now and then. It must be a great comfort to the animal to know it was picked for your one barbecue of the month and not for some ordinary Tuesday lunch.',
  re="An insult that throws away the person's progress."),
 'K2': dict(
  f="The animal on the shelf is already dead, but buying it tells the store to restock, and restocking is the next death. The market counts what was sold and orders more.",
  fe='Explains the mechanism: the purchase counts for the next animal, not the one already dead.',
  m="If you buy it, you killed that animal. The one who pays gives the order, and the butcher just carries it out. That cow's death is on your account the moment the tray reaches the checkout.",
  me="Not quite: that animal died before the purchase. The claim is easy to rebut and hides the right argument, the one about restocking.",
  r="Then go eat roadkill too. It's already dead, and by your logic it would be a waste to leave it there. Or does the \"already dead\" rule only apply when the animal is tasty?",
  re="A taunt that doesn't touch the point about the market: the purchase orders the next animal."),
 'K5': dict(
  f="It's true that almost every product involves some harm in its supply chain. But that doesn't make all choices equal: between beans and meat, the difference in harm is huge. No consumption being perfect doesn't mean none is better.",
  fe='Concedes the partial truth and shows it does not erase the differences.',
  m="It's true that everything under capitalism exploits someone, and that's exactly why veganism is consistent: it's anti-capitalist by nature. Real vegans fight the whole system, and you can't defend animals without fighting the market.",
  me="Veganism doesn't depend on an economic position, and the sentence alienates people who think differently without answering the objection.",
  r="Then stop using your phone, wearing clothes and driving a car, since everything exploits someone. You're talking to me on a device made in who knows what conditions and you want to lecture about consumption. Sort that out first, then criticize others.",
  re='Tu quoque: throws the accusation back instead of answering.'),
 'L12': dict(
  f='Veganism never promised purity: it speaks of avoiding exploitation "as far as is possible and practicable". Greatly reducing harm is different from reducing nothing, and eating plants causes far fewer deaths, including in harvests.',
  fe='Corrects the expectation of purity and shows that reducing harm is the point.',
  m="Yes it is; 100% cruelty-free. No animal suffers or dies for a vegan to eat, dress or have fun, and that's exactly what the word means. Anyone who says otherwise is repeating rumors spread to discredit the movement.",
  me='False, and it confirms the criticism: farming also causes deaths.',
  r="At least we try. What about you? It's easy to point fingers at people who do something while doing nothing yourself. When you've done half of what I do for animals, we can talk about who's cruelty-free.",
  re='Tu quoque: throws the criticism back instead of explaining.'),
}

# Added on 2026-11-14: frequent arguments that are well connected to the ones already in the game, including those
# added to the guide after the first version. Same rule: f sums up the chapter's short answer; m and r are similar in length.
OP.update({
 'A1': dict(
  f='Science has dealt with this: the Cambridge Declaration (2012) and the New York Declaration (2024), signed by researchers, recognize conscious experience in mammals and birds. And "not like we do" doesn\'t help: different isn\'t less.',
  fe='Uses the scientific consensus and also answers the softer version of the argument.',
  m="Animals feel exactly like we do, with no difference at all: a chicken's pain is identical to a person's, and every serious scientist agrees. Anyone who says otherwise simply never studied the subject. Serious science has no doubt about it.",
  me="Overstates: science doesn't say the experience is identical, and defending animals doesn't need it.",
  r="So if I kick your dog it feels nothing? I'll pretend I didn't hear that. Anyone who thinks animals are machines isn't fit to be in this conversation. Serious science has no doubt about this whatsoever.",
  re='Attack and sarcasm instead of the evidence, which exists and is strong.'),
 'A2': dict(
  f='Fish have pain receptors similar to ours, change their behavior for hours after an injury and respond to painkillers. After reviewing more than 300 studies, the United Kingdom recognized octopuses, lobsters and crabs as sentient.',
  fe='Brings the concrete evidence and an institutional consequence of it.',
  m="Fish feel even more pain than we do, because they can't scream and keep it all inside. Several aquarium studies have proven it, and fishermen know it, they just won't admit it. So there's no possible discussion.",
  me="Claims what science doesn't say and invents a \"proof\" that doesn't exist.",
  r="Funny how fish eaters always turn into marine biologists when they need an excuse. If you think fish feel nothing, try hooking your own mouth. I doubt you'd still think fish feel nothing.",
  re='An aggressive taunt that pushes people away instead of explaining.'),
 'A6': dict(
  f="Sometimes humans suffer more, because we anticipate and remember. But often it's the other way round: the animal doesn't understand what is happening and can't console itself. And even if we suffered more, that wouldn't license making another being suffer for a taste.",
  fe="Grants what is true and shows the conclusion doesn't follow.",
  m="No, animals always suffer much more than humans. They feel everything more intensely because they live in the present, so their suffering should count more than any person's. That's what science shows.",
  me='Swaps one generalization for another, equally baseless.',
  r="Here comes the speciesist measuring pain with a ruler. Ever noticed you only use this to justify your plate? If you really thought about it, you wouldn't be saying this. Nobody eats meat because of a pain comparison.",
  re='Accuses the person instead of answering what they said.'),
 'A9': dict(
  f='Superior at what? Dogs smell better, swallows fly better. And even if we were superior at everything, that would give no right to use anyone: an adult outdoes a child at almost everything, and that is why they must protect them.',
  fe='Shows that "superior" depends on the yardstick and that superiority creates duty, not license.',
  m="Humans aren't superior at anything, we're just one more species destroying the planet. Actually, animals are superior to us, because they live in harmony and don't wage wars. Human superiority is pure arrogance.",
  me='Flips the hierarchy instead of showing it gives no right to use anyone.',
  r="Superior? Look at the state of the world and tell me who's superior. People who think like this are the same kind who used that excuse to enslave other humans, and you know it. That logic doesn't hold for a minute.",
  re='Insinuates racism instead of discussing the argument.'),
 'A12': dict(
  f="Preferring our own is normal in real conflicts: in a fire, I may save my child first. But preferring isn't permission to exploit. Loving your own child doesn't license hurting the neighbor's, and nobody is saved when we pick chicken over beans.",
  fe='Accepts legitimate partiality and separates preference from permission to harm.',
  m="It's not natural at all, it's culture. In other eras people put animals first, and ancient peoples lived in harmony with them. The idea of putting humans first was invented by capitalism. Before that, nobody thought this way.",
  me="Makes vague, false historical claims and doesn't answer the point.",
  r="The law of the jungle is natural too, so by that logic anything goes. People who use \"natural\" as an excuse usually just want to keep doing what they already do without thinking. There's nothing natural about paying to kill.",
  re='Ridicules the argument and the person without separating what it gets right.'),
 'A13': dict(
  f='Depends on what "first" means. If it means "the greater harm takes priority", I agree, and the greater harm is almost always on the animal\'s side: a meal against a life. If it means "the human wins even when losing less", that needs explaining.',
  fe='Separates the two meanings and shows where the greater harm lies.',
  m="For me animals always come first. Humans already have people defending them, and animals have no one. That's why real vegans care about animals first and about people afterwards. And that's how it should be.",
  me='Confirms the stereotype that vegans care less about people.',
  r="Funny how humans always come first when it's time to eat, but never when it's time to donate, help or do anything. It's just an excuse not to change anything. And actually caring for people, which would be good, never happens.",
  re='Accuses the person of bad faith without knowing what they do.'),
 'A17': dict(
  f="Nobody can prove who has a soul, so it can't decide who may suffer. And religions usually condemn cruelty: the Catechism of the Catholic Church says it is contrary to human dignity to cause animals to suffer needlessly.",
  fe='Shows the criterion is unprovable and that religion itself condemns cruelty.',
  m="Animals do have souls, and they're even purer than we are because they don't sin. Every sincere religion recognizes it, and anyone who denies it is twisting faith to justify their plate. It's in the sacred texts.",
  me='Answers with another religious claim the person doesn\'t accept.',
  r="Souls? You still believe in that? Arguing from superstition is a waste of time. When you want to use real arguments, we'll talk. Until then, I'd rather talk to people who think with their heads, not with faith.",
  re="Disrespects the person's faith and ends the conversation."),
 'A20': dict(
  f="Not knowing you will die doesn't make death harmless. A baby or someone asleep doesn't know either, and killing them would be very serious. The harm of death is what it takes away: all the life still to come. The animal loses that, knowing it or not.",
  fe='Uses the human test and shows what the harm of death is.',
  m="Animals know perfectly well they're going to die, they smell the blood and understand everything that happens in the slaughterhouse. That's why they cry and try to escape, and it's been proven. Nobody can deny it.",
  me="Overstates what is known and accepts the argument's wrong criterion.",
  r="So I can kill you in your sleep, since you won't know? Think before you speak, because that argument wouldn't fool a child. I've heard plenty of excuses, but honestly that one is among the worst.",
  re='The human test is a good idea, but the tone turns it into a threat and an insult.'),
 'A21': dict(
  f='That settles the question by choice of word. If "person" means self-awareness and plans, newborns aren\'t persons either. If it means having rights, companies are legal persons. Personhood is a tool we grant, not a fact of nature.',
  fe='Shows the definition was chosen to produce the result.',
  m="Animals are persons, and the law already recognizes it in several countries. A pig is a non-human person with the same rights as anyone, and whoever denies it is out of date. Courts have ruled on it many times.",
  me="Claims what the law doesn't say and turns the argument into a dispute over words.",
  r="Here comes the barbecue lawyer. You know very well it's just a technical excuse. If it were a dog, you wouldn't be talking about \"persons\". That definition of person is just a convenient way to keep eating.",
  re='Mocks and accuses of inconsistency without explaining anything.'),
 'B5': dict(
  f='Eggs and milk don\'t kill directly, but their production does. Male chicks of laying breeds are culled at hatching, and a cow only gives milk after giving birth: the calf is taken away, and she is slaughtered when her yield drops.',
  fe='Shows the deaths built into production, with concrete facts.',
  m="Milk has pus in it and eggs are a hen's period. That alone should make anyone stop, and people who still eat them don't know what they're eating. There's no way to defend products like that. Disgusting from start to finish.",
  me='Uses gross, inaccurate claims this guide asks you not to use.',
  r="Of course they don't kill, you just don't want to know what happens to the chicks and calves. Convenient to close your eyes, isn't it? There's a name for that, and it isn't pretty. But you'd rather not know, it's more comfortable.",
  re='Accuses of bad faith instead of informing.'),
 'B6': dict(
  f="Giving life doesn't give the right to take it: parents give their children life and gain no right over it. Someone who doesn't exist loses nothing by not existing; someone who exists loses everything when killed. The line only justifies breeding others to kill.",
  fe='Uses the parent analogy and shows who actually loses.',
  m="Better they didn't exist, because their lives are suffering from start to finish. It would be better if all these farmed species simply disappeared from the planet. Extinction would be best for them, and that's what veganism wants.",
  me='Turns to the extinction of species instead of answering the argument.',
  r="Then let's breed children to sell, since at least they got to exist. See how absurd your argument is? Think before repeating what you heard somewhere. That argument is one of the weakest there is.",
  re='The parallel may be valid, but the tone humiliates.'),
 'B8': dict(
  f='Many animal scientists do take good care, within the goal of their profession, which is raising animals for human use. That is why a "well cared for" chicken is slaughtered at about six weeks. Knowing how to raise animals isn\'t knowing whether it\'s fair to raise them to kill.',
  fe='Acknowledges the technical work and separates competence from moral authority.',
  m="Animal scientists are trained to exploit animals and don't care about them. The whole profession exists to maximize profit from suffering, and every one of them knows it. You can't trust what they say about welfare.",
  me='Generalizes and insults professionals who often genuinely care.',
  r="Then ask the animal scientist if they'd let their daughter live in one of those sheds. Anyone who defends this must be paid to say it. Nobody takes good care of someone they'll kill at six weeks, that's a joke.",
  re='Accuses of corruption without any basis.'),
 'B10': dict(
  f="Some of what's shown is abuse, and some is exactly what the rules allow: gestation crates, castration without anesthesia, culling of chicks. That's not the exception, it's the legal standard. And where there is abuse, those videos are what exposed it.",
  fe='Separates abuse from legal practice and shows the role of the footage.',
  m="Every animal is killed conscious, without stunning, and the videos show exactly what happens on one hundred percent of farms. There are no exceptions at all. Anyone who doubts it can go to any farm and see for themselves.",
  me='States something false that this guide asks you not to use.',
  r="Staged? Sure, the whole industry is a victim of a conspiracy. You'll believe anything that lets you keep eating in peace, won't you? Next you'll say the animals are paid actors.",
  re='Sarcasm and attack, with no data at all.'),
 'C6': dict(
  f="Everyone dying someday doesn't make it acceptable to kill someone early: humans die too, and that never justified killing anyone. And animal farming isn't a natural cycle: animals inseminated, raised in sheds and slaughtered young, by the billions.",
  fe="Separates dying from being killed and shows farming isn't natural.",
  m="The circle of life is when a lion hunts to survive. Humans are herbivores by nature, so eating meat already breaks the cycle entirely, and any biologist will confirm it. Eating meat isn't natural for our species, and the body knows it.",
  me='Uses the false claim that humans are herbivores.',
  r="Circle of life? Then I'll cut your circle short and that's fine, it's all part of it. This argument is so shallow it doesn't even need an answer. Anyone who talks about the circle of life has never set foot in a slaughterhouse, I guarantee it.",
  re='A threat and contempt instead of an argument.'),
 'C8': dict(
  f='In ecology, trophic level measures what a being eats. A 2013 study put the average human level at 2.21, the same as an anchovy, well below orcas and lions. And even at the top, that would describe a diet, not grant rights.',
  fe='Corrects the fact and shows it would carry no moral weight.',
  m="We're actually at the bottom of the food chain, because we're herbivores and our bodies were made to eat plants. The ones at the top are the big predators, never humans. That's basic biology, taught in any school.",
  me='Mixes a real fact with the false claim that we are herbivores.',
  r="Top of the food chain? Drop yourself in the forest with no weapon and no supermarket and see how long you last at the top. That talk comes from someone who's never left the city. Your food chain tops out at the butcher's line.",
  re='Taunts instead of explaining the concept.'),
 'D2': dict(
  f='Many people doing something doesn\'t prove it\'s right: slavery was once accepted by the majority, and those who fought for women\'s suffrage started as a minority. Every moral change began with a few. And the animals, who pay the price, don\'t get a vote.',
  fe="Shows with examples that the majority doesn't decide what's just.",
  m="It's not true that everyone eats it, there are already millions of vegans and soon we'll be the majority. In India, for example, almost everyone has been vegan for centuries. History is on our side, and soon nobody will eat animals.",
  me='Uses false data and accepts the majority criterion.',
  r="Everyone also used to smoke on planes and throw trash in the street. Do you only do what everyone does? Think for yourself for once. Being a sheep isn't an argument, it never was.",
  re='The examples help, but the tone humiliates.'),
 'D9': dict(
  f="Legal isn't the same as right: slavery was legal for centuries, and laws change when society realizes they were wrong. And the law itself already protects animals: cruelty is a crime in many countries.",
  fe='Separates legality from justice and shows the law already recognizes animals.',
  m="Actually it's illegal, it's just that nobody enforces it. Under the Constitution any cruelty to animals is banned, so all animal farming is a crime and should be shut down tomorrow. It's only a matter of time before the courts see it.",
  me='States something legally false.',
  r="The law has also allowed plenty of atrocities, and you'd be defending all of them with that argument. Good thing not everyone thinks like you. The law isn't an excuse for anything, and you know it very well.",
  re='Right example, contemptuous tone.'),
 'D12': dict(
  f="Allowing isn't requiring: your religion allows eating animals, but doesn't demand it. And most religions condemn needless suffering. Today almost all the suffering on farms is needless, and many believers give up eating animals because of their faith.",
  fe='Respects the faith and uses its own values.',
  m="Actually your religion forbids eating meat, you've just misread it. Jesus was vegetarian, and so were the early Christians, it's in the original texts. Just study a bit of the history of religions.",
  me='Makes false historical claims this guide asks you not to use.',
  r="Religion is used to justify anything, wars included. You can't take seriously someone who uses faith as an excuse for their plate. Faith has become the most comfortable excuse there is for not changing.",
  re="Attacks the person's faith instead of engaging with it."),
 'E5': dict(
  f="Science doesn't say what's right, about eating meat or anything else. But it answers the factual questions: do animals feel pain? Yes. The moral question is whether causing that pain needlessly is right. That's ethics, which isn't science, but isn't \"just opinion\" either.",
  fe="Separates fact from value and shows ethics isn't mere taste.",
  m="Veganism is pure science: science proves veganism is right, and all serious research has reached that conclusion. Anyone who disagrees is denying science. It's all in the studies, and numbers don't lie. There's nothing left to discuss.",
  me="Claims what science can't claim, a mistake this guide asks you to avoid.",
  r="So slavery being wrong is just your opinion too, right? With that relativism you can't defend anything. How sad. Think harder before repeating that. Ethics without truth doesn't exist, and you know it.",
  re='The example is good, but the mockery ends the conversation.'),
 'E11': dict(
  f="Liking animals is real and a great start. But pigs, cows and chickens feel pain and fear like the dogs and cats you love. The difference isn't in them, it's in the place habit gave each one. If the love is sincere, it can reach the plate.",
  fe='Acknowledges the affection and invites extending it, without accusing.',
  m="People who love animals don't eat animals, period. Love and meat on the plate don't go together, and if you eat it you don't really love them, you just like the idea of loving. True love has no \"but\". You either love or you don't.",
  me='Turns an invitation into an accusation of falseness.',
  r="\"I love animals, but...\" is the favorite line of people who pay to have them killed. You love animals the way a lion loves a zebra. That \"but\" says everything about your love, doesn't it? Animals feel the difference between love and excuses.",
  re='Insulting irony that pushes away someone already close to changing.'),
 'E13': dict(
  f="Nobody is saying they're bad. Good people also take part in wrong things when those things are normal and nobody questions them. The question isn't about their character, it's about what happens to the animal.",
  fe='Separates the person from the practice and keeps the focus on the animals.',
  m="A truly good person doesn't eat meat. If they do, they might be nice, but real goodness includes every being, so they're not as good as they seem. Their goodness is limited, and that needs saying out loud.",
  me='Judges character, which is exactly what pushes people away.',
  r="A good person who pays to kill? Hitler was kind to his dog too. Selective goodness isn't goodness, it's convenience. Some people are good to their own dog and cruel to everyone else.",
  re='An offensive, disproportionate comparison.'),
 'E14': dict(
  f="Thank you. And if you admire it, you already agree on the most important thing: animals matter. Nobody has to do everything at once. You can start with one meal a day, one product, one week. Want me to show you where I started?",
  fe='Welcomes, recognizes the agreement and offers a concrete first step.',
  m="You just have to want it. If I could, anyone can. There's no \"I can't\", only \"I won't\". Willpower solves everything, and people who don't change just don't care. Just decide once and never look back.",
  me='Ignores real difficulties and sounds like a demand.',
  r="Admiring doesn't save a single animal. While you admire me, they keep dying. Admiration without action is just a polite way of doing nothing. Animals can't eat admiration. Do something.",
  re='Turns a sincere compliment into an accusation.'),
 'F4': dict(
  f="Killing insects by accident while driving is different from paying to have an animal raised and killed. One is accidental and hard to avoid; the other is intentional and easy to avoid. Nobody asks you to avoid every possible harm, only the avoidable ones.",
  fe='Separates accidental from intentional and avoidable from unavoidable.',
  m="Insects don't feel anything, so there's no problem at all in killing them while driving. That's completely different from eating a real animal that feels pain. Comparing the two is absurd and dishonest, and doesn't deserve an answer.",
  me="States a certainty science doesn't have about insects.",
  r="Then let's all stop driving, walking and breathing, because something always dies. That argument is just an excuse not to change anything. Nobody really takes that question seriously, not even the person asking.",
  re='Ridicules the question instead of answering.'),
 'F5': dict(
  f="Protecting yourself from a dengue mosquito or stopping rats from contaminating food is defense against a real threat. Raising an animal to eat isn't: it threatens nobody. And the ideal is prevention: screens, sealing, removing breeding sites.",
  fe='Distinguishes defense from exploitation and shows harm can be reduced.',
  m="I never kill a single mosquito or rat, I'm a real vegan. Anyone who kills any creature, even at home, can't call themselves vegan. That's what consistency is: never killing anything, in any situation, not even in self-defense.",
  me='Creates a purity rule veganism doesn\'t have.',
  r="Was the pig that became bacon attacking you? Were you defending yourself from it? No? Then don't compare the two. What a forced comparison. You don't even believe it yourself. Nobody falls for that, not even you.",
  re='The idea is good, but the tone mocks.'),
 'F6': dict(
  f="You may be right about oysters and mussels: their nervous system is very simple. But in saying so, you accept that what matters is whether the animal feels. And that criterion condemns eating fish, octopuses, chickens and pigs, who do feel.",
  fe='Grants the point and shows the criterion turns against the rest of the plate.',
  m="Oysters and mussels feel pain like any other animal, it's been proven. That's why no vegan should even accept this discussion. Every animal feels the same, from a mussel to a chimpanzee, and doubting it is already speciesism.",
  me="States a certainty science doesn't have, a mistake this guide asks you to avoid.",
  r="Here comes the oyster to rescue the barbecue. Nobody eats only oysters, it's an excuse to justify everything else. Next you'll tell me you're vegan because you eat oysters on Sundays.",
  re='Ridicules the question without using its strong point.'),
 'F7': dict(
  f="Today there's no evidence that plants feel. But if one day we found out, eating plants would still be better, because animals raised for food eat far more plants than we would eat directly.",
  fe='Shows that even granting the hypothesis, the conclusion favors eating plants.',
  m="Plants will never feel anything, it's biologically impossible, and anyone asking is just trying to change the subject. It's a meaningless question from someone looking for any loophole not to change anything.",
  me='Asserts an impossibility and accuses of bad faith.',
  r="Then you eat rocks. Or stop eating. A hypothetical question from someone who doesn't want to think about what happens today, right now, to animals who really feel. It's just a way of dodging the subject.",
  re='Sarcasm that ignores the strong, simple answer.'),
 'F11': dict(
  f='Refusing on principle is consistent. Accepting can be too, if the money saves many more victims. The same question works for any cause: "would you torture someone for a million?". Nobody uses the answer to prove the cause is false.',
  fe='Shows both answers are consistent and the test doesn\'t prove what it claims.',
  m="Never, for no amount of money in the world. Anyone who'd accept isn't really vegan, and I'd never betray my values for money, not even to help anyone. Dirty money never helps any cause, even if it saves many animals.",
  me='Falls into the purity test and dismisses the option of using the money to help.',
  r="Would you kill your dog for a million? If the answer is yes, this conversation is over. What kind of question is that? An absurd question deserves an absurd answer. That's not an argument, it's a distraction.",
  re="Counterattacks instead of showing why the test doesn't work."),
 'G1': dict(
  f='Necessary for whom? People who say this are usually in a supermarket with beans, lentils and chickpeas on the next shelf: there it\'s preference. Where there is no alternative it really is necessity, and the definition of veganism already says "possible and practicable".',
  fe="Acknowledges the real exception and shows it doesn't apply to those who have a choice.",
  m="Nobody needs meat, anywhere in the world, and nobody ever did. Humans are herbivores by nature, and meat has always been a cultural addiction. Every study shows that eating plants is our nature, anywhere in the world.",
  me="Ignores people who truly have no alternative and uses the false claim that we're herbivores.",
  r="Necessary? Look at me, alive and well, without meat for years. If it were necessary, I'd be dead by now. Simple as that. Anyone who says they need it is just too lazy to change their plate, and everyone knows it.",
  re='One personal case doesn\'t answer the argument and sounds arrogant.'),
 'G5': dict(
  f="Plant iron is absorbed at a lower rate, but beans, lentils, tofu and dark leafy greens have plenty, and vitamin C in the same meal increases absorption. And anemia is common on every diet: eating meat doesn't prevent it.",
  fe='Explains the care needed and corrects the idea that meat protects.',
  m="Anemia mostly affects meat eaters, and vegans almost never have iron problems. Meat actually hinders nutrient absorption. Vegans don't need to worry about any of this, just eat well.",
  me='Uses a false claim this guide asks you not to use.',
  r="You're worried about my iron? How sweet. Take care of your cholesterol first, then we'll talk about deficiencies. Funny how nobody asks about the iron of people who live on fast food.",
  re='Irony and counterattack, no information.'),
 'G13': dict(
  f="I'm sorry. But one case doesn't show the cause: often the diet was poorly planned, short on B12 or energy, or there was another problem. People also get sick eating meat. Reviews and nutrition bodies consider a well-planned vegan diet adequate.",
  fe='Welcomes, explains why one case proves no cause and brings the overall evidence.',
  m="That person surely wasn't really vegan, because no vegan gets sick from the diet. It must have been something else, or they ate a lot of junk. Well-done veganism has never harmed anyone, at any age, in any situation.",
  me='Denies the person\'s account and uses the "no true Scotsman" fallacy.',
  r="And I know plenty of people who had heart attacks eating meat. Want to compare cemeteries? Isolated cases prove nothing, least of all yours. People who tell these stories are almost always making them up or exaggerating.",
  re='Has a point, but the aggressive tone erases the empathy needed.'),
 'G14': dict(
  f="What you felt is real, but it usually has simple causes: an abrupt change, too much fiber at once, eating too little or too little variety. With adjustments and time, most of it passes. No study has identified people who need meat by nature.",
  fe='Validates the experience and points to the common causes and the way out.',
  m="Every body adapts, it's psychological. If you felt something, it's because your mind was stuck on meat. You just had to push a little harder. Nobody has a special body that needs meat, that's a pure and simple excuse.",
  me='Dismisses what the person felt.',
  r="Your body adapts to soda, fast food and booze, but not to beans? What a picky body. Your body adapts to everything you want, except what you don't want. What a coincidence.",
  re="Irony that offends and doesn't help."),
 'H1': dict(
  f="About 77% of the world's soy becomes animal feed, and only about 7% becomes tofu, soy milk and other foods. Meat eaters consume far more soy, indirectly. And the main cause of tropical deforestation is pasture.",
  fe='Brings the numbers that turn the argument around.',
  m="Vegans' soy is all organic and from small farmers, and has nothing to do with the Amazon. Only the big meat companies clear forests. Vegans bear no responsibility for this, and anyone who says otherwise means harm.",
  me='Makes a false, sweeping claim.',
  r="Funny how people who eat steak turn environmentalist when it's time to criticize tofu. Get less misinformed and read something before you talk. That argument only serves to let meat eaters off the hook.",
  re='Personal attack without the data that would settle it.'),
 'H11': dict(
  f='Organic and local help on some points, but they don\'t change the essentials: meat emits a lot and uses a lot of land. In Poore and Nemecek\'s data, even the most efficient beef emits more than ten times what the highest-impact peas emit.',
  fe='Acknowledges the merit and shows the proportion that decides it.',
  m="Local, organic meat pollutes even more than industrial meat, and it's the worst option of all for the planet. Buying from small farmers is what destroys the environment most. Nobody should ever buy from small meat producers.",
  me='Overgeneralizes and swaps the ethical question for the environmental one.',
  r="Organic or not, the animal dies just the same. Or do you think the cow is happy because the grass was organic? Organic is just a label so you feel better paying more for the same death.",
  re='Has an ethical point, but the sarcasm gets in the way.'),
 'I1': dict(
  f="Having brought benefits isn't enough: tests on people who can't consent would bring them too, and nobody accepts that. Besides, about 90% of drugs that pass preclinical tests fail in humans, and the US stopped requiring animal tests in 2022.",
  fe="Shows benefit doesn't justify the means and brings the data on effectiveness.",
  m="Animal testing is useless and never saved a single life. Every medical advance came from other methods, and the industry hides it. The studies that say otherwise are all paid for by the pharmaceutical industry.",
  me='States something false this guide asks you not to use.',
  r="Then volunteer for the tests yourself, since you think they're so important. Easy to defend when someone else suffers. Or stop taking medicine, since you think it's so fair. I doubt you would. You don't have the courage to change.",
  re='A taunt instead of an argument.'),
 'I8': dict(
  f="Being tradition doesn't put a practice above the laws against cruelty. England banned hunting with dogs and wild animals in circuses, and Brazil's Supreme Court banned the farra do boi and cockfighting. Festivals can keep the music and food without the suffering.",
  fe='Shows cruel traditions have been banned and that culture can go on.',
  m="That's not culture at all, it's barbarism. People who take part in it are sadists and should be jailed, and whoever defends it is on the side of torture. There's nothing to discuss with people who find this acceptable.",
  me='Insults people instead of separating the festival from the suffering.',
  r="Throwing people to the lions in the Colosseum was culture too. Do you want to go back to that? What kind of culture is this? Culture is no excuse for torture, never was. Honestly, that argument is shameful.",
  re='A disproportionate comparison that only pushes people away.'),
 'J1': dict(
  f="Beans, rice, lentils, chickpeas, potatoes, oats and seasonal vegetables are among the cheapest foods in the store, and they're vegan. The expensive things are the processed products that imitate meat and cheese, which are optional.",
  fe='Separates the cheap basics from the optional processed products.',
  m="Being vegan is expensive, it's for people with money. But it's worth paying more for the animals, so those who can should pay and those who can't will have to manage. It's the price of having a conscience, and worth every cent.",
  me="Confirms the argument's false premise.",
  r="A heart attack is what's expensive. But fine, keep eating meat to save money and spend it later at the hospital. Taking care of your health is cheaper, but you'd rather save now and pay later.",
  re='Uses the threat of disease instead of showing real prices.'),
 'J3': dict(
  f="Changing takes effort at first, like any learning, but then it gets easy. Everyday vegan food can be as quick as food with meat: rice, beans frozen in portions, roasted vegetables, pasta. And you can change gradually.",
  fe='Acknowledges the initial effort and shows it is small and temporary.',
  m="There's nothing hard about it, it's just willpower. People who say they have no time are making excuses, because everyone has time for what they consider important. Those who want to find a way; those who don't find an excuse.",
  me='Ignores the real difficulty and sounds judgmental.',
  r="You have time to watch shows, right? But not to cook some beans? Then the problem isn't time, it's priorities. I've never seen anyone die from cooking beans. You're not busy, you're comfortable.",
  re='Accuses instead of helping.'),
 'J4': dict(
  f="The social cost exists, but it's usually smaller than it looks and shrinks over time. Vegan options keep growing in restaurants and delivery apps, you can bring a dish to the party and research before traveling. People with allergies do this all the time.",
  fe='Acknowledges the difficulty and shows concrete solutions.',
  m="I've never had any problem, anywhere in the world there's a vegan option nowadays. People who say it's hard just didn't look properly. The problem isn't veganism, it's not bothering to look for an option.",
  me="Denies the real experience of many people.",
  r="So your fun is worth more than an animal's life? How selfish. A party without meat won't kill you. The animals didn't ask to die so you could have fun at the weekend. Stay home, then, if it's so hard.",
  re='Moralizes aggressively instead of offering ways out.'),
 'J18': dict(
  f='Calling an idea "leftist" or "globalist" doesn\'t show it\'s wrong. Veganism has advocates on the left, on the right and outside politics. The question is simple: is it fair to make animals suffer and die needlessly?',
  fe='Undoes the guilt by association and returns to the central question.',
  m="Veganism is left-wing, yes, and anti-capitalist by nature. You can't be vegan and defend the market, because all animal exploitation comes from capitalism. Veganism is a political struggle, and you can't depoliticize what is political by nature.",
  me='Confirms the label and pushes away anyone not on the left.',
  r="Globalist agenda? You've been watching too many videos. That's a conspiracy theory from someone with no argument. You believe anything that shows up in your family group chat, don't you? That doesn't even deserve an answer.",
  re='Ridicules the person instead of answering.'),
 'K3': dict(
  f="Paying for an act is taking part in it. Slaughter happens because someone buys the meat; the slaughterhouse worker does, to order, what the consumer doesn't want to see. Among humans, nobody thinks ordering a harm is innocent just because someone else carried it out.",
  fe='Shows the link between buying and slaughter with a clear analogy.',
  m="Eating is exactly the same as killing, there's no moral difference at all. Meat eaters are as much murderers as slaughterhouse workers, and should feel that way. There's no middle ground, and anyone who doesn't accept it is lying to themselves.",
  me='Exaggerates and insults, when showing the participation was enough.',
  r="Then go to the slaughterhouse and kill it yourself, if you're so innocent. I doubt you'd have the guts. Easy to call yourself innocent when someone else does the dirty work for you. Paying is taking part, simple as that.",
  re="A taunt that doesn't explain the responsibility."),
 'K6': dict(
  f="No need to choose. Not eating animals takes no time away from political action, and the two reinforce each other: many protection laws were won by people who also changed their own lives. If you think laws are the way, you can support both.",
  fe='Shows diet and politics add up.',
  m="Laws are useless, because nobody enforces them. The only way to change anything is for each person to go vegan, and politics is a waste of time. What changes the world is each person's choice at the table.",
  me='Dismisses institutional action, which the evidence supports most.',
  r="And do you fight for any law? Or do you just use that as an excuse not to change your plate? How convenient. People who say this have never written a line of a bill in their lives. It's just a pretty way of doing nothing.",
  re='Accuses of hypocrisy instead of answering.'),
 'L1': dict(
  f="Some vegans may be annoying, like in any group. But that changes nothing for the animals. Often just saying you don't eat meat is enough to be seen as \"superior\". The question remains: do animals deserve to be spared?",
  fe='Grants what is possible and turns the focus back to the animals.',
  m="Vegans aren't annoying, they're the only people who really care. People who find us annoying feel guilty and don't want to hear the truth. Annoying is refusing to hear the truth about what you eat every day.",
  me='Confirms the impression of superiority the person is criticizing.',
  r="And what about people who make vegan jokes at every barbecue? At least we don't need to kill anyone to have fun. We're only annoying because you can't stand hearing what's done to animals.",
  re='Hits back and reinforces the stereotype.'),
 'L6': dict(
  f="The link between meat and masculinity is cultural, and research has documented it. But masculinity isn't a moral or nutritional criterion. There are vegan strength athletes, fighters and bodybuilders, and courage is also changing when you realize something is wrong.",
  fe='Explains where the idea comes from and shows it decides nothing.',
  m="Vegans are stronger and more virile than meat eaters, and they're better in bed too. Eating meat is what lowers testosterone, it's been proven. Meat eaters are the ones who grow weak and sick over time, and everyone knows it.",
  me='Answers with exaggerated claims this guide asks you not to use.',
  r="You need a steak to prove you're a man? What fragile masculinity. That says more about you than about meat. A real man doesn't need to prove anything at a barbecue. Think about it. It's what people afraid of looking weak say.",
  re='Mocks the person and makes the conversation worse.'),
 'L11': dict(
  f="Even if I were inconsistent, what I say wouldn't become false: the animal suffers just the same. And veganism never promised purity: it speaks of avoiding exploitation \"as far as is possible and practicable\". There's no impact-free phone today, but there is an animal-free lunch.",
  fe='Undoes the tu quoque and explains what veganism actually asks.',
  m="My phone is from a sustainable company and I never buy anything with any impact. A real vegan avoids everything, and I do. Anyone who can't manage that shouldn't even call themselves vegan, because they're fooling themselves.",
  me='Promises an impossible purity the accusation will knock down.',
  r="And you use a phone and eat meat, so you're a hypocrite twice over. Shall we start with your plate? Or with your car, your clothes and your electricity bill? Pick one. Hypocrisy for hypocrisy, yours is bigger.",
  re='Tu quoque thrown back, explaining nothing.'),
 'L21': dict(
  f="Those comparisons can hurt, and it's fair to ask for care or avoid them. Pointing out similar mechanisms, like treating sentient beings as things, isn't saying the victims are worth the same. And the case for animals doesn't depend on those analogies.",
  fe="Respects the sensitivity and shows the cause doesn't depend on the comparison.",
  m="Animal farming is worse than the Holocaust, because it kills far more. The numbers speak for themselves, and anyone offended just won't face the truth. You can't soften the truth to please anyone.",
  me='Makes the comparison insensitively, which pushes away and hurts.',
  r="What's offensive is what happens to animals, not my comparison. If words offend you more than deaths, that's your problem. Animals don't care about your discomfort, they care about what's done to them.",
  re='Disregards the real pain those comparisons can cause.'),
 'L30': dict(
  f="That's not an argument, it's a provocation. When we feel pressured, we tend to do the opposite. But the one who pays the \"compensation\" isn't me, it's the animals. Whenever you want to really talk about them, I'm here.",
  fe="Doesn't take the bait and leaves the door open.",
  m="Eat double, it makes no difference. Every vegan already saves 200 animals a year, so even if you eat more I still save far more than you kill. Trying to provoke me won't work, the balance is still positive for the animals.",
  me='Uses a number this guide asks you not to use and joins the contest.',
  r="Very mature. Eating double just to provoke. That's why you can't talk to people like you. That shows exactly the level of meat eaters. The animals deserve better people than that.",
  re='Answers a provocation with another and ends the conversation.'),
 'M1': dict(
  f="What you felt was real. But were you able to look into the cause? It's almost always too few calories, missing B12 or poor planning, and all of that can be solved without animal products. If you want to try again, with support, I can help.",
  fe='Welcomes, looks into the cause and offers help.',
  m="You did it wrong, because nobody feels bad being vegan. If you'd done it right, you'd be fine. It's a lack of information and willpower. Veganism never harms anyone if it's done properly.",
  me='Blames the person and denies what they felt.',
  r="Feeling bad is an excuse to kill animals? Then when I have a headache I can do whatever I want too. Everyone has their excuses. Animals also feel bad in the slaughterhouse, you know? But that doesn't bother you, does it?",
  re='Dismisses a real health problem.'),
 'N1': dict(
  f="The animals we see are the ones that survived. In most species, almost all young die of hunger, cold, disease or predation soon after birth. If it happened to human children, we'd call it a catastrophe, not freedom.",
  fe='Shows the reality of nature with the human test.',
  m="Nature is perfect and balanced, wild animals live in harmony and never suffer like farmed ones. That's why we need to leave nature alone. Everyone knows animals are happier living free in nature.",
  me='Repeats the idyllic view the argument itself relies on.',
  r="So you want to end nature so nobody suffers? What a delusion. Sentientists are even crazier than vegans. Leave the animals alone and stop inventing problems where there are none. Nobody can stand this anymore.",
  re="It's an attack, not an answer, and it distorts the position too."),
})
